// The system that runs on a machine: the target, over the box next door.
//
// No `generate` attribute, and that is forced rather than stylistic: the
// grammar admits a `system_target` only in a module that generates nothing. So
// the process, the machine it runs on and the box they host are declared in
// `robot.clk`, and this file names the target over them and emits only the
// process description and per-domain configs the runner reads at start-up.
//
// What comes out of here is what a process needs on disk to run at all: the
// process description each of the two processes is pointed at, the per-domain
// channel allocations and metadata beside them, and the `LogWriterConfig` the
// logger box binds -- which is generated from the policies below and is the
// whole of what an online run records.
//
// The deterministic runner's own two policies are deliberately absent: what to
// publish out of an input log cannot apply to a system whose producers are live
// sockets.
//
// The policies live here rather than beside the channels they bind because
// `channel_policy` generates nothing, and a module that generates C++ may not
// import one that generates no targets.
//
// The set is exactly the set `system_sim.clk` logs, minus the two channels that
// exist only in the simulated system. One vocabulary of record for both, so a
// hardware log and a scenario log answer the same questions and the analyzer
// over one reads the other.
//
// The logger's own three channels are logged too, by the framework's own policy
// module for them: a run's records say whether the thing writing them was
// keeping up.

use cogs::motion;
use cogs::robot;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_channel_policies;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::channel_policy::{ChannelLoggingPolicy, LogType};
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::channel_type::{ChannelType};

// The machine's heartbeat: one sample per cycle, always.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverPose
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What it was asked to hold next.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverCmd
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the gate did that the sample stream does not show.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverEvt
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the session asked the driver for, and what came back: the answer to one
// transaction, and the rotating read of the status registers.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::SessionCmdChan
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverAuxOut
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverHealth
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What was asked of the machine, kept beside what it did. In this system the
// asking is the wake cog next door rather than a log the runner replays.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::ScriptsIn
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the session made of a script: the schedule the mover streams, whole and
// once per change.
//
// Persistent: the newest message is the whole of what the machine is under
// command to do, so a logger attaching after the engagement took hold is handed
// the schedule it missed rather than nothing.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::ScheduleChan
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
    channel_type = ChannelType::persistent;
}

// What the decision tick raised.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::TickFaults
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

// What the session said about the session: the whole story so far, republished
// whenever a row is added to it.
//
// Persistent rather than regular, for the same reason the driver's status
// record is: a logger attaching to a regular channel starts at the write head
// and reads nothing the ring already holds, so a row published before it
// attached is lost. On a persistent channel it is handed the newest retained
// message at attach -- and the newest message here is the whole story, so that
// one message is the run's narration entire.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::ReportsOut
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
    channel_type = ChannelType::persistent;
}

// What the driver says about its whole run, republished on a cadence.
//
// Persistent rather than regular, which is the whole point of the record: a
// logger attaching to a regular channel starts at the write head and reads
// nothing the ring already holds, so every fact published before it attached is
// lost. On a persistent channel it is handed the newest retained message at
// attach -- and the newest message here is cumulative, so that one message is
// the run so far. Nothing the run is verified from can then go missing because
// of when the logger happened to start.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::DriverStatus
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
    channel_type = ChannelType::persistent;
}

// Where the head was, once per sample.
policy ChannelLoggingPolicy for motion::Estimates
{
    log_type = LogType::event;
}

system_target motion_robot
{
    box: robot::System;
}
